text:
# fix(res.send): send the bytes covered by a DataView

`res.send()` recognized a `DataView` as binary (`ArrayBuffer.isView()` is
true), but later called `Buffer.from(dataView)`, which produces an empty
`Buffer`. A non-empty view therefore returned HTTP 200 with
`Content-Length: 0` and an empty body.

## Root cause

`lib/response.js` in the object case:

```js
} else if (ArrayBuffer.isView(chunk)) {
  if (!this.get('Content-Type')) {
    this.type('bin');
  }
} else {
  return this.json(chunk);
}
```

When `chunk` is a `DataView`, this sets `Content-Type: application/octet-stream`
but leaves `chunk` as the `DataView`. The length/body handling then falls into:

```js
} else {
  chunk = Buffer.from(chunk, encoding)  // DataView -> empty Buffer
  ...
}
```

`Buffer.from(dataView)` yields an empty buffer, so the view's bytes are
silently dropped. The same `Buffer.from(typedArray)` behavior is correct for
`Uint8Array` (it materializes the view correctly), but a `DataView` is not a
typed array — it only references a slice of a parent `ArrayBuffer`.

## Fix

After the existing `this.type('bin')` guard (which preserves a manually-set
`Content-Type`), materialise a `DataView` by its exact byte range instead of
by the `DataView` itself:

```js
} else if (util.types.isDataView(chunk)) {
  // A DataView references a slice of a parent ArrayBuffer, and
  // Buffer.from(dataView) yields an empty Buffer. Materialise the exact byte
  // range it covers so the response carries the same bytes a Uint8Array would.
  chunk = Buffer.from(chunk.buffer, chunk.byteOffset, chunk.byteLength);
}
```

This preserves:
- the byte order and the exact range (`byteOffset` .. `byteOffset + byteLength`),
- the existing `Content-Type` when the user set one,
- the ETag when one is configured,
- the `Uint8Array` path (which already worked via `Buffer.from(typedArray)`).

## Regression tests

New describe block `res.send(DataView)` in `test/res.send.js` with 6 cases, all
passing:

- sends the exact byte range covered by the view
- does not override an existing `Content-Type`
- preserves the ETag when one is set
- sends the bytes of a view that does not cover its whole buffer
- sends cross-realm `DataView`s unchanged
- does not affect plain `ArrayBuffer` behaviour

## Validation

- `npx mocha test/res.send.js` → 82 passing (the 6 new DataView tests included)
- `npm test` → 1267 passing
- `npm run lint` → clean (no errors)

## Related

- Closes #7510
- Related to the DataView-specific problem discussed in #7433 (closed without
  merging by its author) and the bare `ArrayBuffer` support work in #7362.

## Summary

Fixes repro 1 from #37761, the `.map()` one. #37779 covers the other three (forward refs / self-referencing closures), and this is meant to sit alongside it rather than replace it.

```jsx
function List({items}) {
  const [last, setLast] = useState(0);
  function open(item) {
    setLast(Date.now() + item.length); // flagged as "Cannot call impure function during render"
  }
  return (
    <div>
      {items.map(item => (
        <button key={item} onClick={() => open(item)}>{last}</button>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

What's going on: `items.map(cb)` emits `MutateTransitiveConditionally cb`, and the callback has a plain `Capture cb <- open` from its CreateFunction. So in `InferMutationAliasingRanges.mutate()` the transitive mutation walks back through the callback's captures, hits `open`, and treats it as called. But the callback never calls `open`. It only builds the `onClick` arrow, which gets frozen into JSX. You can see this in the callback's own `aliasingEffects`, which have no mutation of `open`.

The fix threads a `maybeCalled` flag through the mutate queue. When we go backwards through a function's captures, it stays true only if that function's aliasing effects actually mutate the captured value. Mutable ranges aren't affected, since the traversal itself is unchanged. This only decides whether `appendFunctionErrors` runs. If a function has no aliasing effects yet, it falls back to the old behavior.

I made the same change in the Rust port so the two stay in sync.

Heads up: this touches the same `if (node.value.kind === 'Function' && ...)` check as #37779, so whichever lands second will need a small rebase. Happy to do that on my side.

## How did you test this change?

New fixtures:
- `impure-fn-call-in-handler-created-in-map`: the repro from the issue, no longer errors
- `error.invalid-impure-fn-called-from-map-callback`: the `.map()` callback *does* call the impure helper during render, which is still reported

Ran on a Windows checkout with LF line endings and compared against main:
- `yarn snap`: same failure list as main apart from the new fixture now passing. The pre-existing local failures are fbt/ssr/etc. stuff unrelated to this.
- `yarn snap --rust`: same, plus the new fixture. The Rust output matches TS.
- `yarn workspace babel-plugin-react-compiler lint`, `tsc --noEmit`, prettier, `cargo fmt --check` are clean
